\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  \textcolor {MidnightBlue}{Albert: {Our stuff is 128 bits of security? (Once we add grinding?) How many queries?}} *= we are not using virtualization of XOR. The committed and opened witness size is 4x larger than it could be. \textcolor {MidnightBlue}{Albert: {Missing costs for F2Z PIOP. Note these are negligible for the hash benches though, and likely quite less than the grand product cost in other benches. All these were obtained asking AI to run benches. For the final paper we should make sure we are doing fair and correct benching (e.g.\ I'm not sure memory consumption is measured the same way across benches, especially for Binius, which is max RSS), but this helps get a rough idea how F2Z performs relatively to other systems.}} Prover\_ time(ms)/Verifier\_time(ms)/Proof\_size(KB)/Peak\_memory(MB). Integer mult measure one million u16 x u16 -> u32 mults. F2Z-PCS does not include PIOP costs -- these are negligible in the hash benches (linear constraints), but should be accounted for in the other benches (degree 2 constraints).Ran in MacBook Air M4 16GB.  \indent \hspace  {1cm} Flock's verifier seems a bit broken in general. Flock's Blake3 prover for small number of hashes seems broken too. I modified Flock's Ligerito config so that it produces smaller proofs at very little extra cost. For Blake3 we couldn't do $2^7$ hashes becase Flock errors out on $2^7$ hashes \textcolor {MidnightBlue}{Albert: {todo: make SHA also do $2^8$ instead of $2^7$}}   \indent \hspace  {1cm} F2Z-PCS hash performance was ``proxied'' as follows: for SHA-256, $2^{14+k}$ witness corresponds to $2^k$ hashes. Better arithmetization may be possible. We *DID NOT* leverage virtualization of XOR, which allows to run Ligerito for $2^{12+k}$ witness size, rather than $2^{14+k}$ (The GKR grand product still would use use $2^{14+k}$ factors). \textcolor {MidnightBlue}{Albert: {VERY IMPORTANT: It is possible that the GKR grand product can be optimized in this setting. Right now we are just proving a $2^{14+k}$ on unstructured bits, but in reality many of these bits are linear combinations in $\ensuremath  {\mathbb  {F}}_2$ of witness bits.}}  \indent \hspace  {1cm} Binius64 achieves the highest number of mults per second at $2^{17}$ mults. Binius64 at multiplication of 16bit integers pays a penalty because the system uses 64-bit words by default, so it either uses a 64 bits per 16bit witness element and range check the word, or pack different 16 and 32 bit integers into the same 64 bit word, but then it has to relate the product of the 64 bit words to the products of the original words (or unpack them as 64 bit words before multiplying). OTOH doing more Binius-native mults (32x32 ->64 bits) does not perform amazingly either \textcolor {MidnightBlue}{Albert: {This most likely heavily disproves my belief that Binius64 and F2Z-SNARK are similar in integer multiplication. This is a good showcase of the flexibility of arithmetizing the way we do: we don't need to use predefined word sizes: here our R1CS is just reconstruction of integers via linear combinations with powers of twos, and Hadamard product (note that we are not measuring the cost of the PIOP in F2Z! But it should be cheaper than the grand product, and amenable to some better optimization (univariate skip e.g.). But in any case, I'm not even sure the issue is the rigidity of the arithmetization in Binius64, since 32x32->64 is Binius friendly as far as I understand, and performance is mid}} }}{6}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
