ear combinations in $\FF _2$ of witness bits.}\\ \indent \hspace {1cm} Binius64 achieves the highest number of mults per second at $2^{17}$ mults. Binius64 at multiplication of 16bit integers pays a penalty because the system uses 64-bit words by default, so it either uses a 64 bits per 16bit witness element and range check the word, or pack different 16 and 32 bit integers into the same 64 bit word, but then it has to relate the product of the 64 bit words to the products of the original words (or unpack them as 64 bit words before multiplying). OTOH doing more Binius-native mults (32x32 ->64 bits) does not perform amazingly either \albert {This most likely heavily disproves my belief that Binius64 and F2Z-SNARK are similar in integer multiplication. This is a good showcase of the flexibility of arithmetizing the way we do: we don't need to use predefined word sizes: here our R1CS is just reconstruction of integers via linear combinations with powers of twos, and Hadamard product (note that we are not measuring the cost of the PIOP in F2Z! But it should be cheaper than the grand product, and amenable to some better optimization (univariate skip e.g.). But in any case, I'm not even sure the issue is the rigidity of the arithmetization in Binius64, since 32x32->64 is Binius friendly as far as I understand, and performance is mid}}{table.caption.5}{}}
